## 3. The Method
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

"The Method" means a way of running the rounds of a multiplayer game with simultaneous moves on a decidable temporal
logic. It is described in three layers. The first layer is the Method proper; the second is the three ways of executing
it; the third is the substrate, which is not the author's.

### 3.1 The method (layer one)

The features of this layer depend on the logic only. They do not depend on how the engine is hosted (in a process, in a
subprocess, behind a service, or otherwise), and they do not depend on the rules of any particular game.

**F1 — The rule is a formula, and the formula is what runs.**
The round function — how the inputs of all parties and the state after the previous round determine the outputs and the
state after this round — is written as a formula in a decidable temporal logic, or as several formulas with a declared
wiring (section 3.2). The round is settled by evaluating that text: in the executions X1 and X2, no separately written
program states an outcome of the round; in the execution X3, a program runs the game, and a move or an outcome stands
only if the evaluation of the formula admits it (section 3.2).

**F2 — A function, with nothing left free.**
The formula determines every output as a function of the inputs of the round and the previous state. Every state stream
has an initial condition in the formula; inputs outside the intended range are made impossible by the type of the input
or are given an outcome by a line of the formula; what follows when several players finish in one round, when a party
does not open, and when the game has ended is stated as lines of the formula and is not chosen by a host program; no
connection between parts has a default value.

**F3 — Properties as queries on the same object.**
Stated properties of the rule (for example: an outcome stays in its range; with the other contributions and the previous
state fixed, the outcome determines a party's own contribution; two versions of a line are or are not the same function)
are posed to the engine as queries on the formula that is executed, each with its expected verdict and, wherever one
exists, a control query that must come out the other way.

**F4 — Recomputation is re-evaluation.**
A round is checked by evaluating the formula again on the recorded inputs of the round and the previous state, on the
engine build named for the game, and comparing every output. The check starts from the inputs of the round, not from
recorded intermediate values.

**F5 — Bound inputs and a chain of round records.**
Each party's input to a round (contribution and move) is fixed by a salted commitment before any input is opened; the
operator contributes as well and opens last; what follows from a withheld opening is a rule of the game and stands in
the formula; each round has a signed record with the openings and all outputs of the step, and every record carries the
hash of its predecessor, so that a single step can be recomputed by a third party from two consecutive records and the
game header.

**F6 — A common random value from contributions of all parties, with carried state.**
The random value of a round is computed inside the formula from contributions of all parties together with a state
carried over from the previous round. Two instances of this feature are

    seed[t] = c1[t] ^ c2[t] ^ ... ^ cn[t]            (contributions combined)
    next[t] = mix(next[t-1] + seed[t])               (carried state, a bijective mixing step)
    roll[t] = map(next[t])                           (a mapping to the outcome range)

and

    roll[t] = ((d1[t] + ... + dn[t] + dB[t] + (next[t-1] % m)) % m) + 1,   di[t] = ci[t] % m

where `mix` and `map` are fixed in the formula, `m` is the number of outcomes, and `dB` is the operator's contribution.

The features describe a use of such a logic. The logic, its decidability, and the engine that evaluates the formula and
decides the queries are not the author's (section 2).

### 3.2 Three executions (layer two)

The Method has three executions. All three are executions of the Method; none replaces another. Which of X1 and X2 fits
depends on the size of the game and on the engine build; in X3 a program runs the game and the formula, in the form of
X1 or of X2, validates it.

**X1 — One specification.**
The whole round is one specification that carries its state: the state of the game is a set of output streams with
initial conditions and transitions that read the previous time point.

**X2 — A network of specifications.**
The round is an acyclic network of part specifications. Each part is evaluated on its own: a part that reads the
previous time point runs as one timeline, and a part that reads only the current time point can be evaluated by any
worker. The outputs of parts are handed to the inputs of other parts, and the state is handed in as inputs.

**W — The wiring table.**
In the network, the connections are a declared object: a table that names, for every input of every part, its one source
— an input of the round, a state value, a constant, or an output of another part. The table and the part formulas are
hashed together, and the hash stands in the header of the game. A check of a round evaluates the parts from the inputs
of the round and hands the values on by the table.

**X3 — A program runs the game, and the formula validates.**
A program that is not the formula runs the game: it collects the inputs of the parties and carries out the phases of a
round. The formula — one specification as in X1, or a network as in X2 — validates every move, or every round, by
evaluation on the inputs of the round and the state recorded after the previous round. The formula is the governing rule
of the round: a move or an outcome stands only if the evaluation of the formula admits it, and where a round is checked,
its outcome is the result of evaluating the formula on the recorded inputs of the round and the state recorded after the
previous round (F4).

The author's first texts describe this form as well. The author's white paper of 13 December 2025 describes a game
server that collects the commitments and runs the phases of a round while the logic validates all moves, and releases
v1.1 and v2.0 of this license named the loop "Commit → Reveal → Validate → Execute".

Releases v1.1 (19 December 2025) and v2.0 (August 2026) of this license described the network execution in the form in
which the author first released it: a random value with carried state as in F6; persistent engine processes in the
engine's interactive `run always` mode, so that state is preserved between steps; decomposition of the round into
several independent services, each with its own engine process; and a round in which all players submit for the same
round and all moves take effect together. That form is the execution X2 with the connections held in a host program
instead of a declared table. It remains an execution of the Method.

### 3.3 The substrate (layer three)

The substrate is Tau Language in a named build. It belongs to IDNI AG and is not part of the Method (section 2).

### 3.4 What is not the Method

The rules of a single game are not the Method. The board, the pieces, the rule by which a piece is captured, the rule by
which a piece enters, the prize, and every other rule of a particular game can be exchanged without leaving the Method,
and none of them is claimed by this license as such. The single techniques listed in section 9 are not the Method
either.

### 3.5 Scope of "the Method" for the purpose of section 8

A system uses the Method if its rounds are settled as in F1 in execution X1 or in execution X2 (with a declared wiring
table or with the connections held in a host program), or if its rounds are run by a program that the formula validates,
every move or every round, as in execution X3; and it realizes in addition at least two of the features F2, F3, F4, F5,
F6 and W.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## 4. What is and is not in the Materials, and what is not asserted
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

So that this license describes what exists:

- The Method Materials in this release consist of two small teaching examples of a random value computed from the
  contributions of several players: `casino.tau`, which carries no state between rounds, and
  `temporal_state_machine.tau`, which takes the previous state as an input. Neither binds inputs, neither carries its
  state inside the specification, and neither is a complete game.
- A complete round specification, part specifications of a network, a wiring table, queries and records are not part of
  this release.
- This license describes the Method by its features. It does not assert that any feature has been carried out for any
  particular game or deployment. What has been run, what is designed and not run, and what remains trusted is stated in
  the author's paper on the Method (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23163642, which resolves to its current version) for the
  executions X1 and X2 that it describes, with its evidence and its limits; it is not stated here, for any of the three
  executions.
- This license makes no statement that any system — the author's or anyone else's — is fair, secure, or free of operator
  influence.

